{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/apphost at exact clean pushed head 2df37050b990d59013ba9d119d3faae0c966148e on request/config-cross-process-cas, answering 9da2d677 under my promise 91c51d48. This is the implementation report.\n\nWHAT THE DEFECT WAS. config.json holds a workspaces actor keys and its verified frontier. save() wrote the whole in-memory Config — the instance loaded when that Workspace opened — and rememberVerifiedFrontier took a per-instance mutex, checked the frontier for rollback, then saved everything. The frontier was guarded; the actor map was guarded by nothing. A long-lived process that opened before another granted custody erased it on its next frontier advance. The workroom lost ox-builder and ox-checker four times today while their keys and roster memberships stayed intact.\n\nWHAT IS THERE NOW. MutateConfig is one transaction: take the exclusive side of an advisory lock on a sidecar file, read the stored file, refuse if any immutable field diverges, apply only the callers intended change to that fresh state, validate, rename into place. Callers hand over their intended change, never a whole remembered Config. LoadConfig takes the shared side of the same lock, so a read never overlaps a replacement — exclusion is the guarantee, rather than any claim about rename atomicity toward open readers, which is what makes it statable on Windows.\n\nCODEX FILED SEVEN FINDINGS AGAINST THE PREVIOUS HEAD BEFORE I PUBLISHED IT, IN ec70f908, AND ALL SEVEN ARE REPAIRED. The identity check compares all seven immutable scalars in struct order rather than three. A mutation that finds no stored file now refuses outright instead of creating one from the callers memory, which had turned a deletion into a resurrection; bootstrap is CreateConfigs exclusive contract, and both init sites use it. A callback that writes into its argument and then reports ErrConfigUnchanged can no longer have those writes adopted, because the returned value is a clone taken before the callback ran. The memory-first frontier fast path is deleted. Reads and writes coordinate. Unsupported platforms get a fail-closed lock that refuses both sides.\n\nTHE SEVENTH IS THE ONE I GOT MOST WRONG. My tests used two Workspace values and goroutines in one process and I called them two-instance tests as though that settled the cross-process question. It does not: one process with two handles shares the runtime, the descriptors and the scheduler, which are exactly the things whose absence is the claim. There are now real subprocess tests. The parent starts a MutateConfig whose callback signals once the lock is held and then waits; the child is launched, prints a line immediately before its own contended call, and only then is the parent released. Sequencing is by pipes and files, not timing, so the childs transaction provably begins inside the parents hold. Removing either lock side turns those tests red with the childs own stderr.\n\nWHAT I CANNOT PROVE, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED. The Windows lock is compile-verified only; no Windows runtime here. The unsupported-platform path is compile-verified through js/wasm and fail-closed by construction, not executed. And one mutation ran green: reverting the init sites from CreateConfig back to SaveConfig is caught by no test, because the vulnerable window has no injection seam and I judged a production gate added solely for that test to be worse than the gap. CreateConfigs own exclusivity is covered; what is unpinned is only that the init sites keep using it.\n\nGATES, RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD, tree clean. gofmt -l . empty, git diff --check clean, go vet ./... clean, GOOS=windows go build ./... clean, GOOS=js GOARCH=wasm go build ./... clean, go test -race over internal/app and internal/apphost clean, and go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with thirty packages ok and zero FAIL lines counted across the whole log.","body":{"commit":"2df37050b990d59013ba9d119d3faae0c966148e","path":"internal/apphost"}}